{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_path_recursive(int n, int adj[100][100], int u, int v) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][i] == 1) {\n\t\t\tif (has_path_recursive(n, adj, i, v)) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][2] = 1; adj[2][3] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << has_path_recursive(4, adj, 0, 3) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool has_path_bfs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int start, int end) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start);\n\tvisited[start] = true;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return true;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tvisited[v] = true;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(4);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(3);\n\tstd::cout << has_path_bfs(4, adj, 0, 3) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version uses an adjacency matrix for a sparse graph and lacks a visited check, leading to infinite loops or  complexity in cycles. The optimized version uses an adjacency list and BFS with a visited array, ensuring  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_reachable(int u, int adj[50][50], int n) {\n\tint count = 1;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][v] == 1) {\n\t\t\tcount += count_reachable(v, adj, n);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint graph[50][50] = {0};\n\tgraph[0][1] = 1; graph[1][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_reachable(0, graph, 3) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited, int& count) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tcount++;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) dfs(v, adj, visited, count);\n\t}\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(2);\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tdfs(0, adj, visited, count);\n\tstd::cout << count << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses a dense adjacency matrix for sparse data and fails to track visited nodes, causing exponential time or stack overflow on cycles. The optimized approach uses an adjacency list and a visited set to achieve linear  performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortest_path_naive(int start, int end, int n, int adj[20][20]) {\n\tif (start == end) return 0;\n\tint min_dist = 1e7;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[start][i] > 0) {\n\t\t\tmin_dist = std::min(min_dist, adj[start][i] + shortest_path_naive(i, end, n, adj));\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn min_dist;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint g[20][20] = {0};\n\tg[0][1] = 5; g[1][2] = 10; g[0][2] = 20;\n\tstd::cout << shortest_path_naive(0, 2, 3, g) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint dijkstra(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1e7);\n\tstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<std::pair<int, int>>> pq;\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tpq.push({0, start});\n\twhile (!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint d = pq.top().first, u = pq.top().second;\n\t\tpq.pop();\n\t\tif (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\t\tif (u == end) return d;\n\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[u] + edge.second < dist[edge.first]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[edge.first] = dist[u] + edge.second;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({dist[edge.first], edge.first});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[end];\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back({1, 5}); adj[1].push_back({2, 10}); adj[0].push_back({2, 20});\n\tstd::cout << dijkstra(0, 2, n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe naive recursion performs a brute-force exhaustive search of all paths, resulting in exponential  time. Dijkstra's algorithm with a priority queue and adjacency list reduces this to , significantly improving scalability for weighted graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool check_cycle_recursive(int u, int n, int adj[10][10], std::vector<int> path) {\n\tfor (int x : path) if (x == u) return true;\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][v] == 1) {\n\t\t\tif (check_cycle_recursive(v, n, adj, path)) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][2] = 1; adj[2][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << check_cycle_recursive(0, 3, adj, {}) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_cycle_dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1; // Visiting\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && has_cycle_dfs(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2; // Visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(0);\n\tstd::vector<int> state(n, 0);\n\tstd::cout << has_cycle_dfs(0, adj, state) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code uses a vector to track the current path for cycle detection, leading to  lookups at every step and exponential path exploration. DFS coloring (Three-state DFS) with an adjacency list reduces the time complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_components(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint components = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!visited[i]) {\n\t\t\tcomponents++;\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> stack = {i};\n\t\t\twhile(!stack.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();\n\t\t\t\tvisited[u] = true;\n\t\t\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (adj[u][v] == 1 && !visited[v]) stack.push_back(v);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn components;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_components(3, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstruct UnionFind {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent;\n\tint count;\n\tUnionFind(int n) : parent(n), count(n) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n\t}\n\tint find(int i) {\n\t\tif (parent[i] == i) return i;\n\t\treturn parent[i] = find(parent[i]);\n\t}\n\tvoid unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint root_i = find(i), root_j = find(j);\n\t\tif (root_i != root_j) {\n\t\t\tparent[root_i] = root_j;\n\t\t\tcount--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n};\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tUnionFind uf(n);\n\tuf.unite(0, 1);\n\tstd::cout << uf.count << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version uses a nested loop over an adjacency matrix () inside a search, making it inefficient for sparse graphs. Union-Find with path compression provides a near-constant time  for merging components, which is significantly faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_edges(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j] != 0) total++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 10; adj[50][51] = 5;\n\tstd::cout << count_edges(100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_edges_list(const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\ttotal += neighbors.size();\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 100;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back({1, 10});\n\tadj[50].push_back({51, 5});\n\tstd::cout << count_edges_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe adjacency matrix requires  time to count edges regardless of the graph's density. Using an adjacency list reduces this to , which is much faster for sparse graphs where ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_node_with_max_neighbors(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint max_n = -1, node = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j] != 0) current++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current > max_n) { max_n = current; node = i; }\n\t}\n\treturn node;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[0][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_node_with_max_neighbors(100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_max_deg(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint max_n = -1, node = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < adj.size(); i++) {\n\t\tif ((int)adj[i].size() > max_n) {\n\t\t\tmax_n = adj[i].size();\n\t\t\tnode = i;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn node;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(100);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << find_max_deg(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version scans a dense matrix () to calculate degrees. The adjacency list stores the degree explicitly via the size of each neighbor vector, allowing the search to complete in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_bipartite_naive(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (1 << n); i++) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> colors(n);\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) colors[j] = (i >> j) & 1;\n\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (adj[u][v] && colors[u] == colors[v]) valid = false;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (valid) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 1}, {1, 1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << is_bipartite_naive(3, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool is_bipartite_bfs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (color[i] == -1) {\n\t\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(i); color[i] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (color[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcolor[v] = 1 - color[u];\n\t\t\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t\t\t} else if (color[v] == color[u]) return false;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tadj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(1);\n\tadj[0].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << is_bipartite_bfs(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version uses a brute-force bitmask approach, checking all  colorings in  time. Using BFS/DFS for 2-coloring with an adjacency list reduces the time complexity to a linear ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_self_loop(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (i == j && adj[i][j] != 0) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[5][5] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << has_self_loop(100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_self_loop_list(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < adj.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (int neighbor : adj[i]) {\n\t\t\tif (neighbor == i) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(100);\n\tadj[5].push_back(5);\n\tstd::cout << has_self_loop_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version uses a nested loop over an adjacency matrix (), checking every cell. The adjacency list version only checks actual edges, leading to  complexity, and the self-loop check can be further optimized by only checking the -th row in  in a matrix."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_connected_components(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tbool found = false;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j]) { found = true; break; }\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (!found) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_connected_components(3, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) dfs(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint components = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!visited[i]) {\n\t\t\tcomponents++;\n\t\t\tdfs(i, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tstd::cout << components << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses a flawed heuristic that only checks immediate neighbors, failing to detect multi-node components. The optimized version uses standard DFS-based component traversal, ensuring correct results in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_min_dist_unweighted(int n, int adj[50][50], int start, int end) {\n\tif (start == end) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> q = {start};\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1000);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q[0]; q.erase(q.begin());\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[u][v] == 1 && dist[v] == 1000) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push_back(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[end];\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_min_dist_unweighted(3, adj, 0, 2) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint bfs_shortest_path(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int start, int end) {\n\tif (start == end) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tif (v == end) return dist[v];\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << bfs_shortest_path(n, adj, 0, 2) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses `std::vector::erase(begin)` which is , leading to  for the queue operations alone. Using `std::queue` with an adjacency list reduces the complexity to  for unweighted shortest paths."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_dag_naive(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> q = {i};\n\t\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\tint u = q.back(); q.pop_back();\n\t\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (adj[u][v]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (v == i) return false;\n\t\t\t\t\tif (!vis[v]) { vis[v] = true; q.push_back(v); }\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << is_dag_naive(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool is_dag_kahn(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> in_degree(n, 0);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) in_degree[v]++;\n\t}\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (in_degree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n\tint count = 0;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tcount++;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (--in_degree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count == n;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << is_dag_kahn(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version runs a full traversal from every node () to detect cycles. Kahn's algorithm (Topological Sort) identifies cycles in  by checking if all nodes can be processed based on in-degrees."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_all_edges(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j]) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[49][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_all_edges(50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_edges_fast(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& row : adj) count += row.size();\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[49].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << count_edges_fast(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe matrix approach iterates over  entries even for sparse graphs. The adjacency list stores only existing edges, allowing the count to be determined in  by summing the sizes of the vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid get_transpose(int n, int adj[10][10], int trans[10][10]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ttrans[j][i] = adj[i][j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {0, 0}}, trans[10][10];\n\tget_transpose(2, adj, trans);\n\tstd::cout << trans[1][0] << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> transpose_list(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> trans(n);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) trans[v].push_back(u);\n\t}\n\treturn trans;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1);\n\tauto trans = transpose_list(n, adj);\n\tstd::cout << trans[1][0] << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe adjacency matrix transpose always takes  time. Using an adjacency list reduces the complexity to , as we only visit and redirect existing edges."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint get_degree_naive(int node, int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint degree = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[node][i] != 0) degree++;\n\t}\n\treturn degree;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[0][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << get_degree_naive(0, 50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint get_degree_fast(int node, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\treturn (int)adj[node].size();\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << get_degree_fast(0, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nCalculating the degree in an adjacency matrix is  as it requires scanning an entire row. In an adjacency list, the degree is pre-stored as the size of the vector for that node, reducing the operation to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_connected_naive(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> q = {i};\n\t\t\tbool found = false;\n\t\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.back(); q.pop_back();\n\t\t\t\tif (u == j) { found = true; break; }\n\t\t\t\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++) if(adj[u][k] && !vis[k]) { vis[k]=true; q.push_back(k); }\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (!found) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << is_connected_naive(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nbool is_connected_optimized(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tif (n == 0) return true;\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tdfs(0, adj, vis);\n\tfor (bool v : vis) if (!v) return false;\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tstd::cout << is_connected_optimized(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code checks connectivity between every pair using  traversals, resulting in . A single DFS traversal from node 0 can verify connectivity in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_self_loops(int n, int adj[20][20]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (i == j && adj[i][j]) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[20][20] = {0};\n\tadj[1][1] = 1; adj[2][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_self_loops(20, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_self_loops_fast(int n, int adj[20][20]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[i][i]) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[20][20] = {0};\n\tadj[1][1] = 1; adj[2][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_self_loops_fast(20, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version uses a nested loop () to check diagonal elements. Since self-loops only occur where , a single loop through the diagonal () is sufficient and much faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool find_cycle_naive(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> q = {i};\n\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\tint u = q.back(); q.pop_back();\n\t\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (adj[u][v]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (v == i) return true;\n\t\t\t\t\tq.push_back(v);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_cycle_naive(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool dfs_cycle(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1; // visiting\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && dfs_cycle(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2; // visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::vector<int> state(n, 0);\n\tbool cycle = false;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(state[i] == 0 && dfs_cycle(i, adj, state)) cycle = true;\n\tstd::cout << cycle << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe naive version performs an exhaustive search from every node, potentially never terminating on cycles. The optimized DFS coloring approach detects back-edges in  time, providing a robust and efficient solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_leaf_nodes(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint leaves = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint count = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j]) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (count == 1) leaves++;\n\t}\n\treturn leaves;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_leaf_nodes(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_leaves_list(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint leaves = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tif (neighbors.size() == 1) leaves++;\n\t}\n\treturn leaves;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(2);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << count_leaves_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version scans every row of an adjacency matrix (). An adjacency list stores the degree of each node directly as the size of its neighbor vector, reducing the search to  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_path_exists(int u, int v, int n, int adj[10][10], std::vector<int> path) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < path.size(); i++) if (path[i] == u) return false;\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][k] && is_path_exists(k, v, n, adj, path)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {0, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << is_path_exists(0, 1, 2, adj, {}) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool dfs_path(int u, int v, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!vis[neighbor] && dfs_path(neighbor, v, adj, vis)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1);\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tstd::cout << dfs_path(0, 1, adj, vis) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version uses a linear search in a vector for cycle detection and passes the vector by value, causing  work per call and exponential total time. Using a boolean visited array and adjacency list reduces complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint get_in_degree(int node, int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint degree = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[i][node]) degree++;\n\t}\n\treturn degree;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[1][0] = 1; adj[2][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << get_in_degree(0, 100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> compute_all_in_degrees(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> in_degree(n, 0);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) in_degree[v]++;\n\t}\n\treturn in_degree;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 100;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[1].push_back(0); adj[2].push_back(0);\n\tauto in_degrees = compute_all_in_degrees(n, adj);\n\tstd::cout << in_degrees[0] << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nCalculating in-degree for a specific node in an adjacency matrix requires  time. By pre-calculating all in-degrees in  using an adjacency list, lookups become , which is beneficial for algorithms like topological sorting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_shortest_path_weighted(int n, int adj[10][10], int s, int t) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1000);\n\tdist[s] = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (adj[u][v] && dist[u] + adj[u][v] < dist[v]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + adj[u][v];\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[t];\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 5; adj[1][2] = 10;\n\tstd::cout << find_shortest_path_weighted(3, adj, 0, 2) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint dijkstra(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj, int s, int t) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1e7);\n\tstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<std::pair<int, int>>> pq;\n\tdist[s] = 0; pq.push({0, s});\n\twhile (!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint d = pq.top().first, u = pq.top().second; pq.pop();\n\t\tif (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[u] + edge.second < dist[edge.first]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[edge.first] = dist[u] + edge.second;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({dist[edge.first], edge.first});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[t];\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back({1, 5}); adj[1].push_back({2, 10});\n\tstd::cout << dijkstra(n, adj, 0, 2) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original Bellman-Ford style approach on an adjacency matrix runs in . Dijkstra's algorithm with a priority queue and adjacency list optimizes weighted shortest path calculation to , which is superior for graphs with non-negative weights."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_cycle_undirected_naive(int u, int p, int n, int adj[10][10], std::vector<bool> vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][v]) {\n\t\t\tif (!vis[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tif (has_cycle_undirected_naive(v, u, n, adj, vis)) return true;\n\t\t\t} else if (v != p) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 1}, {1, 1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << has_cycle_undirected_naive(0, -1, 3, adj, std::vector<bool>(3, false)) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool dfs_cycle_undirected(int u, int p, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!vis[v]) {\n\t\t\tif (dfs_cycle_undirected(v, u, adj, vis)) return true;\n\t\t} else if (v != p) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0); adj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(1); adj[0].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(0);\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tstd::cout << dfs_cycle_undirected(0, -1, adj, vis) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version passes the `visited` vector by value in each recursive call, leading to  space and time per call. Passing by reference and using an adjacency list ensures an  complexity for cycle detection."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid bfs_all_levels(int n, int adj[100][100], int start) {\n\tstd::vector<int> q = {start};\n\tstd::vector<int> level(n, -1);\n\tlevel[start] = 0;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q[0]; q.erase(q.begin());\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[u][v] && level[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tlevel[v] = level[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push_back(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1;\n\tbfs_all_levels(100, adj, 0);\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid bfs_optimized(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int start) {\n\tstd::vector<int> level(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tlevel[start] = 0;\n\tq.push(start);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (level[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tlevel[v] = level[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 100;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1);\n\tbfs_optimized(n, adj, 0);\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses `std::vector::erase` on the first element, which is an  operation, resulting in  for the traversal loop. Switching to `std::queue` and an adjacency list achieves the optimal  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_all_triangles(int n, int adj[20][20]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (adj[i][j] && adj[j][k] && adj[k][i]) count++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count / 6;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[20][20] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1]=adj[1][0]=adj[1][2]=adj[2][1]=adj[2][0]=adj[0][2]=1;\n\tstd::cout << count_all_triangles(3, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_triangles_list(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (v > u) {\n\t\t\t\tfor (int w : adj[v]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (w > v) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfor (int back : adj[w]) if (back == u) count++;\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tadj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(1);\n\tadj[2].push_back(0); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << count_triangles_list(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe adjacency matrix approach checks all  combinations, which is extremely slow. Using an adjacency list and ordered node traversal reduces the search space to , where λ is the average degree, making it much more efficient for sparse graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_regular(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint first_deg = 0;\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[0][j]) first_deg++;\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current_deg = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) current_deg++;\n\t\tif (current_deg != first_deg) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << is_regular(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_regular_list(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tif (adj.empty()) return true;\n\tsize_t deg = adj[0].size();\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tif (neighbors.size() != deg) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(2);\n\tstd::cout << is_regular_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version requires  time to calculate the degrees of all nodes in an adjacency matrix. The adjacency list stores degrees as vector sizes, allowing the regularity check to complete in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_outdegree(int node, int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint out = 0;\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\tif (adj[node][j]) out++;\n\t}\n\treturn out;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[0][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_outdegree(0, 50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint outdegree_fast(int node, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\treturn (int)adj[node].size();\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << outdegree_fast(0, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nIn an adjacency matrix, calculating the out-degree requires scanning an entire row (). For an adjacency list, the out-degree is simply the size of the vector for that node, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_sink_node(int u, int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[u][j]) return false;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (i != u && !adj[i][u]) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << is_sink_node(0, 50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_sink_optimized(int u, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, const std::vector<int>& in_degree) {\n\tif (!adj[u].empty()) return false;\n\treturn in_degree[u] == n - 1;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 50;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tstd::vector<int> in_degree(n, 0);\n\tstd::cout << is_sink_optimized(0, n, adj, in_degree) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version re-scans the matrix repeatedly to check sink conditions. By maintaining in-degrees and using an adjacency list, we can check if a node is a universal sink (out-degree 0 and in-degree N-1) in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_cliques_size_2(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (i < j && adj[i][j]) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_cliques_size_2(100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_edges_fast(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < adj.size(); u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (v > u) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(100);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << count_edges_fast(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nCounting size-2 cliques (edges) in an adjacency matrix takes  even if the graph is almost empty. The adjacency list allows the iteration to be limited to actual edges, providing  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool is_eulerian_circuit(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint deg = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) deg++;\n\t\tif (deg % 2 != 0 || deg == 0) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 1}, {1, 1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << is_eulerian_circuit(3, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nbool is_eulerian_optimized(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (adj[i].size() % 2 != 0) return false;\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint start = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!adj[i].empty()) { start = i; break; }\n\tif (start == -1) return true;\n\tdfs(start, adj, vis);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!adj[i].empty() && !vis[i]) return false;\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0); adj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(1); adj[2].push_back(0); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << is_eulerian_optimized(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version only checks node degrees but ignores connectivity, which is necessary for a Eulerian circuit. The optimized version checks both even degrees () and graph connectivity () using an adjacency list and a single DFS traversal."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_adjacent(int u, int v, int adj[100][100]) {\n\treturn adj[u][v] != 0;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[5][10] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << is_adjacent(5, 10, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nbool is_adjacent_fast(int u, int v, const std::vector<std::unordered_set<int>>& adj) {\n\treturn adj[u].count(v) > 0;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::unordered_set<int>> adj(100);\n\tadj[5].insert(10);\n\tstd::cout << is_adjacent_fast(5, 10, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nWhile an adjacency matrix provides  lookup, it consumes  space. An adjacency list of `std::unordered_set` provides average  edge lookups with  space, making it better for large, sparse graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_path_distance_recursive(int u, int v, int n, int adj[10][10], int dist) {\n\tif (u == v) return dist;\n\tif (dist > n) return 1000;\n\tint min_d = 1000;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][i]) {\n\t\t\tint d = find_path_distance_recursive(i, v, n, adj, dist + 1);\n\t\t\tif (d < min_d) min_d = d;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn min_d;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << find_path_distance_recursive(0, 1, 2, adj, 0) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid floyd_warshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j])\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> dist(n, std::vector<int>(n, 1000));\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) dist[i][i] = 0;\n\tdist[0][1] = 1; dist[1][0] = 1;\n\tfloyd_warshall(n, dist);\n\tstd::cout << dist[0][1] << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original recursive version uses a brute-force depth-first search without visited nodes, causing exponential  complexity. Floyd-Warshall computes all shortest paths in , offering a vastly more efficient and deterministic runtime."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_complete_graph(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (i != j && !adj[i][j]) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << is_complete_graph(50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_complete_optimized(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[i].size() != (size_t)(n - 1)) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 50;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tstd::cout << is_complete_optimized(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version checks all  pairs in the adjacency matrix. The optimized version simply checks if every node has exactly  neighbors in  time using the adjacency list's pre-computed vector sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_path_recursive_naive(int u, int v, int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][i]) {\n\t\t\tif (has_path_recursive_naive(i, v, n, adj)) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[1][0] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << has_path_recursive_naive(0, 1, 50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool has_path_bfs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int start, int end) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(start); vis[start] = true;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return true;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!vis[v]) { vis[v] = true; q.push_back(v); }\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 50;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << has_path_bfs(n, adj, 0, 1) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe naive recursion lacks a visited check and explores all paths, leading to exponential time or infinite loops. BFS with an adjacency list and a visited array identifies a path in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_components_brute(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint comps = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> seen(n, false);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!seen[i]) {\n\t\t\tcomps++;\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) seen[j] = true;\n\t\t\tseen[i] = true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn comps;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << count_components_brute(50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nint count_components_dfs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!vis[i]) {\n\t\t\tcount++;\n\t\t\tdfs(i, adj, vis);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 50;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tstd::cout << count_components_dfs(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version only marks immediate neighbors, failing to find multi-node components. Proper DFS traversal on an adjacency list ensures all nodes in a component are visited, correctly identifying components in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_node_with_max_outdegree(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tint max_out = -1, node = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) current++;\n\t\tif (current > max_out) { max_out = current; node = i; }\n\t}\n\treturn node;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1; adj[0][2] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << find_node_with_max_outdegree(100, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint max_out_fast(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint max_out = -1, node = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < adj.size(); i++) {\n\t\tif ((int)adj[i].size() > max_out) {\n\t\t\tmax_out = adj[i].size();\n\t\t\tnode = i;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn node;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(100);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << max_out_fast(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code scans every entry of an adjacency matrix () to determine degrees. An adjacency list provides the out-degree for each node in  via vector size, enabling an  search."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_bipartite_recursive(int u, int n, int adj[10][10], std::vector<int>& color, int c) {\n\tcolor[u] = c;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][v]) {\n\t\t\tif (color[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tif (!is_bipartite_recursive(v, n, adj, color, 1 - c)) return false;\n\t\t\t} else if (color[v] == c) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 1}, {1, 1, 0}};\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tstd::cout << is_bipartite_recursive(0, n, adj, color, 0) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool is_bipartite_bfs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (color[i] == -1) {\n\t\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(i); color[i] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (color[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcolor[v] = 1 - color[u];\n\t\t\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t\t\t} else if (color[v] == color[u]) return false;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 3;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0); adj[1].push_back(2); adj[2].push_back(1); adj[2].push_back(0); adj[0].push_back(2);\n\tstd::cout << is_bipartite_bfs(n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses an adjacency matrix ( per search) and a recursive DFS that can fail for disconnected components. The BFS approach with an adjacency list correctly and efficiently colors the entire graph in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_isolated_naive(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tbool empty = true;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) { empty = false; break; }\n\t\tif (empty) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[100][100] = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << has_isolated_naive(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_isolated_list(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tif (neighbors.empty()) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(2);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::cout << has_isolated_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nChecking for an isolated node in an adjacency matrix requires  time in the worst case. In an adjacency list, we can check if any node's neighbor vector is empty in  time, offering a significant speedup for sparse graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_all_loops(int n, int adj[20][20]) {\n\tint loops = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[i][i]) loops++;\n\t}\n\treturn loops;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[20][20] = {0};\n\tadj[5][5] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << count_all_loops(20, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_loops_list(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < adj.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[i]) if (v == i) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(20);\n\tadj[5].push_back(5);\n\tstd::cout << count_loops_list(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version works correctly in  for a matrix. The adjacency list version is , which might be slower if . However, for very sparse graphs with high node counts, an adjacency list is generally preferred for overall space efficiency."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_node_degree_k(int n, int adj[50][50], int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint d = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (adj[i][j]) d++;\n\t\tif (d == k) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tstd::cout << has_node_degree_k(50, adj, 0) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool check_degree_k(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int k) {\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tif ((int)neighbors.size() == k) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tstd::cout << check_degree_k(adj, 0) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nCalculating degrees using an adjacency matrix costs  time as you must scan all cells. The adjacency list allows degree checks in  because the degree is simply the size of each node's neighbor vector."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint total_degree_sum(int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tint sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (adj[i][j]) sum++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn sum;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][1] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << total_degree_sum(50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint sum_degrees_fast(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint sum = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) sum += (int)neighbors.size();\n\treturn sum;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tadj[0].push_back(1);\n\tstd::cout << sum_degrees_fast(adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe adjacency matrix requires scanning  entries to sum the degrees. The adjacency list stores edges directly, allowing the sum to be calculated in  by aggregating the sizes of the neighbor vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_path_shorter_than_k(int u, int v, int k, int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tif (k < 0) return false;\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][i] && is_path_shorter_than_k(i, v, k - 1, n, adj)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {0, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << is_path_shorter_than_k(0, 1, 2, 2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool path_k_bfs(int u, int v, int k, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(u); dist[u] = 0;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint curr = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (curr == v) return dist[curr] <= k;\n\t\tif (dist[curr] >= k) continue;\n\t\tfor (int neighbor : adj[curr]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[neighbor] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[neighbor] = dist[curr] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(neighbor);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n); adj[0].push_back(1);\n\tstd::cout << path_k_bfs(0, 1, 2, n, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original recursive solution performs an exhaustive search of all paths, leading to exponential  complexity. BFS with an adjacency list calculates the shortest path in  and checks if it meets the length requirement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool is_cyclic_recursive_naive(int u, int n, int adj[10][10], std::vector<bool> vis) {\n\tif (vis[u]) return true;\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) if (adj[u][v] && is_cyclic_recursive_naive(v, n, adj, vis)) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << is_cyclic_recursive_naive(0, 2, adj, std::vector<bool>(2, false)) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_cycle_dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && has_cycle_dfs(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2;\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint n = 2;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n); adj[0].push_back(1); adj[1].push_back(0);\n\tstd::vector<int> state(n, 0);\n\tstd::cout << has_cycle_dfs(0, adj, state) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version passes the `visited` vector by value, causing  overhead per recursive call. The optimized DFS uses a single state array and an adjacency list to detect cycles in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint find_max_path_distance_brute(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tint max_d = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> q = {i};\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> d(n, -1);\n\t\t\td[i] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q[0]; q.erase(q.begin());\n\t\t\t\tif (d[u] > max_d) max_d = d[u];\n\t\t\t\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++) if(adj[u][k] && d[k]==-1) { d[k]=d[u]+1; q.push_back(k); }\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_d;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << find_max_path_distance_brute(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint graph_diameter(int n, int adj[10][10]) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> dist(n, std::vector<int>(n, 100));\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tdist[i][i] = 0;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(adj[i][j]) dist[i][j] = 1;\n\t}\n\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++)\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\tint max_d = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) for(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(dist[i][j] < 100) max_d = std::max(max_d, dist[i][j]);\n\treturn max_d;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[10][10] = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}};\n\tstd::cout << graph_diameter(2, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses  BFS traversals with  erasure overhead, leading to . The Floyd-Warshall algorithm computes all-pairs distances in , providing a more efficient way to find the graph diameter."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool has_neighbor_greater_than(int u, int val, int n, int adj[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][j] && j > val) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tint adj[50][50] = {0};\n\tadj[0][10] = 1;\n\tstd::cout << has_neighbor_greater_than(0, 5, 50, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool has_neighbor_fast(int u, int val, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (neighbor > val) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(50);\n\tadj[0].push_back(10);\n\tstd::cout << has_neighbor_fast(0, 5, adj) << std::endl;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe original version scans an entire row in an adjacency matrix (). The adjacency list only iterates over existing neighbors, providing  time, which is much faster for sparse nodes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\n// Check if graph has a cycle using brute force path enumeration\nbool hasCycleBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\t\tif (e.first == i && e.second == i) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool hasCycleDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1; // Visiting\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && hasCycleDFS(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2; // Visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nbool checkGraph(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) adj[e.first].push_back(e.second);\n\tstd::vector<int> state(n, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (state[i] == 0 && hasCycleDFS(i, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe optimization replaces a naive edge scan with a DFS-based coloring algorithm. It improves complexity from  or worse to  by tracking node states (unvisited, visiting, visited) to detect back-edges efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAllPairs(int n, int dist[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] != INT_MAX && dist[k][j] != INT_MAX) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][j] > dist[i][k] + dist[k][j])\n\t\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] != INT_MAX && dist[k][j] != INT_MAX) {\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe optimized code correctly implements the Floyd-Warshall algorithm by placing the intermediate node loop () as the outermost loop. This ensures that the shortest path between any two nodes is correctly updated using all possible intermediate vertices, maintaining  while fixing the logic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint unweightedShortestPath(int start, int end, int n, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> edges) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);\n\tfor(auto p : edges) adj[p.first].push_back(p.second);\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::pair<int, int> curr = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(curr.first == end) return curr.second;\n\t\tfor(int neighbor : adj[curr.first]) q.push({neighbor, curr.second + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint optimizedBFS(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe original code lacked a visited check, leading to exponential complexity  in cyclic graphs. The optimized BFS uses a distance array as a visited set, ensuring each vertex is processed once for a linear  runtime."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nbool isConnected(int n, int matrix[100][100], int u, int v) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][i] == 1) {\n\t\t\tif (isConnected(n, matrix, i, v)) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent;\n\tDSU(int n) : parent(n) { std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0); }\n\tint find(int i) {\n\t\tif (parent[i] == i) return i;\n\t\treturn parent[i] = find(parent[i]);\n\t}\n\tvoid unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint root_i = find(i), root_j = find(j);\n\t\tif (root_i != root_j) parent[root_i] = root_j;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool optimizedCheck(int u, int v, DSU& dsu) {\n\treturn dsu.find(u) == dsu.find(v);\n}\n---\nThe recursive search without a visited array results in infinite loops or redundant computations. Using Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression provides near-constant time  connectivity queries after an  construction."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nbool hasNegativeCycle(int n, std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1e9);\n\tdist[0] = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n * n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[e.u] + e.w < dist[e.v]) dist[e.v] = dist[e.u] + e.w;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nbool bellmanFord(int n, const std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, 1e9);\n\tdist[0] = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (const auto& e : edges) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[e.u] != 1e9 && dist[e.u] + e.w < dist[e.v])\n\t\t\t\tdist[e.v] = dist[e.u] + e.w;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor (const auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif (dist[e.u] != 1e9 && dist[e.u] + e.w < dist[e.v]) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code ran for  iterations unnecessarily. The optimized Bellman-Ford algorithm correctly terminates after  iterations and performs one final pass to detect negative cycles in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid dfsBrute(int u, int target, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tif (u == target) return;\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif (e.first == u) dfsBrute(e.second, target, edges);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid dfsOptimized(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) dfsOptimized(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe input code repeatedly scans the entire edge list for every recursion, leading to  or worse. Using an adjacency list and a visited array reduces complexity to  by eliminating redundant scans and cycles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <climits>\n\nint naiveDijkstra(int start, int end, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint u = -1;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (!visited[j] && (u == -1 || dist[j] < dist[u])) u = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tvisited[u] = true;\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[u][v] != 0) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[u] + matrix[u][v] < dist[v]) dist[v] = dist[u] + matrix[u][v];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[end];\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <climits>\n\nstruct Edge { int to, weight; };\n\nint fastDijkstra(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<Edge>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<>> pq;\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tpq.push({0, start});\n\twhile (!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint d = pq.top().first, u = pq.top().second; pq.pop();\n\t\tif (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[u] + edge.weight < dist[edge.to]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.weight;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({dist[edge.to], edge.to});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[end];\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is inefficient for sparse graphs. Using a priority queue with an adjacency list reduces complexity to , which is significantly faster when  is much smaller than ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint countEdges(int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[i][j] == 1) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countEdgesOptimized(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tcount += neighbors.size();\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nScanning an adjacency matrix takes  regardless of the number of edges. Using an adjacency list allows counting edges in  time by summing the sizes of the neighbor vectors, which is much faster for sparse data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid getNeighbors(int u, int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][i] == 1) {\n\t\t\t// do something with i\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid getNeighborsOptimized(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t// do something with v\n\t}\n}\n---\nQuerying neighbors in an adjacency matrix is always , which is expensive for large, sparse graphs. An adjacency list provides direct access to neighbors in  time, yielding significant speedups for vertices with few connections."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isPath(int u, int v, int n, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> edges) {\n\tif (u == v) return true;\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif (e.first == u) return isPath(e.second, v, n, edges);\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool isPathBFS(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start);\n\tvisited[start] = true;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return true;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tvisited[v] = true;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original recursive search is prone to infinite loops on cycles and has  worst-case time. The BFS optimization uses a visited set and adjacency list for a guaranteed  traversal, providing safety and performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findSet(int i, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tif (parent[i] == i) return i;\n\treturn findSet(parent[i], parent);\n}\n\nvoid unionSets(int i, int j, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tint root_i = findSet(i, parent);\n\tint root_j = findSet(j, parent);\n\tif (root_i != root_j) parent[root_i] = root_j;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint findSetOptimized(int i, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tif (parent[i] == i) return i;\n\treturn parent[i] = findSetOptimized(parent[i], parent);\n}\n\nvoid unionSetsOptimized(int i, int j, std::vector<int>& parent, std::vector<int>& rank) {\n\tint root_i = findSetOptimized(i, parent);\n\tint root_j = findSetOptimized(j, parent);\n\tif (root_i != root_j) {\n\t\tif (rank[root_i] < rank[root_j]) parent[root_i] = root_j;\n\t\telse if (rank[root_i] > rank[root_j]) parent[root_j] = root_i;\n\t\telse { parent[root_i] = root_j; rank[root_j]++; }\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original Union-Find can degrade to  per operation (a chain). Path compression and union-by-rank optimize this to , effectively making queries and updates nearly constant time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint countUniqueEdges(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::set<std::pair<int, int>> unique_edges;\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tunique_edges.insert(e);\n\t}\n\treturn unique_edges.size();\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nstruct pair_hash {\n\tstd::size_t operator()(const std::pair<int, int>& p) const {\n\t\treturn std::hash<int>{}(p.first) ^ (std::hash<int>{}(p.second) << 1);\n\t}\n};\n\nint countUniqueOptimized(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::unordered_set<std::pair<int, int>, pair_hash> unique_edges;\n\tfor (const auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tunique_edges.insert(e);\n\t}\n\treturn unique_edges.size();\n}\n---\n`std::set` uses a balanced BST, leading to  insertion time. `std::unordered_set` uses a hash table, offering  average time complexity, which is significantly faster for large edge lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint findMinDist(int n, int dist[100], bool visited[100]) {\n\tint min = INT_MAX, min_index;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++)\n\t\tif (!visited[v] && dist[v] <= min)\n\t\t\tmin = dist[v], min_index = v;\n\treturn min_index;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\n// Use priority queue to find minimum distance node\n// This is the core of Dijkstra's optimization\n// ... inside the algorithm ...\nstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<>> pq;\n// pq.push({dist[start], start});\n---\nThe linear scan to find the minimum distance vertex takes , making Dijkstra . A priority queue (min-heap) reduces this to , making the overall graph traversal ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid transpose(int n, int matrix[100][100], int res[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tres[j][i] = matrix[i][j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> transposeAdj(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> rev(n);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\trev[v].push_back(u);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rev;\n}\n---\nThe matrix transpose takes  regardless of density. Transposing an adjacency list only visits existing edges, resulting in  time, which is vastly superior for the sparse graphs typically found in real-world applications."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool isBipartiteNaive(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\t// Brute force checking all possible 2-colorings\n\t// O(2^N) complexity\n\treturn false; // complex placeholder\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool isBipartiteBFS(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (color[i] == -1) {\n\t\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(i); color[i] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (color[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcolor[v] = 1 - color[u];\n\t\t\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t\t\t} else if (color[v] == color[u]) return false;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nBipartiteness testing via brute force coloring is . Using BFS/DFS with two colors allows checking for odd cycles in , providing a polynomial time solution to the problem."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nvoid sortEdges(std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < edges.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < edges.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (edges[i].w > edges[j].w) std::swap(edges[i], edges[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nvoid fastSortEdges(std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tstd::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](const Edge& a, const Edge& b) {\n\t\treturn a.w < b.w;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe original bubble sort has  complexity, which is devastating for Kruskal's algorithm on large graphs. `std::sort` uses Introsort, providing  complexity, which is necessary for efficient minimum spanning tree construction."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint getInDegree(int v, int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tint deg = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[i][v]) deg++;\n\t}\n\treturn deg;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> getAllInDegrees(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> inDegree(n, 0);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tinDegree[v]++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn inDegree;\n}\n---\nCalculating in-degree for a single node in a matrix is . To calculate all in-degrees, it's . An adjacency list allows calculating all in-degrees in  by iterating over every edge exactly once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <cmath>\n\nfloat getAvgDegree(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[i][j]) total++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (float)total / n;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nfloat getAvgDegreeFast(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tsize_t total = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& list : adj) total += list.size();\n\treturn (float)total / n;\n}\n---\nIn an adjacency matrix, calculating the average degree takes . With an adjacency list, it's  (or  if size isn't cached), which is significantly more efficient for large graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isIsolated(int u, int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][i] || matrix[i][u]) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isIsolatedOptimized(int u, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, const std::vector<int>& inDegree) {\n\treturn adj[u].empty() && inDegree[u] == 0;\n}\n---\nThe original code performs a  scan. By pre-calculating in-degrees in  and using an adjacency list for out-degrees, isolated node checks become  per query."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid removeEdge(int u, int v, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tfor (auto it = edges.begin(); it != edges.end(); ) {\n\t\tif (it->first == u && it->second == v) it = edges.erase(it);\n\t\telse ++it;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nvoid fastRemove(int u, std::vector<std::unordered_set<int>>& adj, int v) {\n\tadj[u].erase(v);\n}\n---\nRemoving an edge from a vector of pairs is . Using a vector of `unordered_set` as an adjacency list allows for  average time edge removal and  edge existence checks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findCenter(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint degree = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) if (matrix[i][j]) degree++;\n\t\tif (degree == n - 1) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint findCenterFast(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<int> degree(n + 1, 0);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tdegree[e.first]++;\n\t\tdegree[e.second]++;\n\t}\n\tfor (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n\t\tif (degree[i] == n - 1) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nFinding a graph center in a matrix is . For a star graph, we can find the center in  time by counting degrees, which is much more efficient as  in such cases."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid reachability(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\t\t\tif (matrix[j][i] && matrix[i][k])\n\t\t\t\t\tmatrix[j][k] = 1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <bitset>\n\nvoid bitsetTransitiveClosure(int n, std::vector<std::bitset<100>>& reach) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (reach[j].test(i)) reach[j] |= reach[i];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original Floyd-Warshall reachability is . By using `std::bitset`, we can utilize word-level parallelism for the bitwise OR, effectively reducing the constant factor by 32 or 64 ()."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid levelOrder(int root, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(root);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[u][v]) q.push(v);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid optimizedLevelOrder(int root, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(root);\n\tvisited[root] = true;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tvisited[v] = true;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe input code has no visited check and uses an  inner loop. The optimized version uses a visited array to prevent infinite cycles and an adjacency list to iterate only over existing edges, achieving ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nint findMSTBrute(int n, std::vector<Edge> edges) {\n\t// Tries all spanning trees\n\t// O(E^V) placeholder\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> p;\n\tDSU(int n) : p(n) { std::iota(p.begin(), p.end(), 0); }\n\tint find(int i) { return p[i] == i ? i : p[i] = find(p[i]); }\n\tvoid unite(int i, int j) { p[find(i)] = find(j); }\n};\n\nint kruskal(int n, std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tstd::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](Edge a, Edge b) { return a.w < b.w; });\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tint mst_w = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif (dsu.find(e.u) != dsu.find(e.v)) {\n\t\t\tdsu.unite(e.u, e.v);\n\t\t\tmst_w += e.w;\n\t\t\tif (++count == n - 1) break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn mst_w;\n}\n---\nKruskal's algorithm with DSU and efficient sorting reduces MST finding from exponential to , which is optimal for edge-list based graph representations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isClique(int n, int matrix[100][100], std::vector<int> nodes) {\n\tfor (int i : nodes) {\n\t\tfor (int j : nodes) {\n\t\t\tif (i != j && matrix[i][j] == 0) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <bitset>\n\nbool isCliqueOptimized(int n, const std::vector<std::bitset<100>>& adj, const std::vector<int>& nodes) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nodes.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nodes.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (!adj[nodes[i]].test(nodes[j])) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nThe original code checks  pairs. While the complexity remains  where  is the clique size, using `std::bitset` for the adjacency matrix significantly improves cache locality and allows for future bitwise optimizations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid findTriangles(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\tfor (int k = j + 1; k < n; k++)\n\t\t\t\tif (matrix[i][j] && matrix[j][k] && matrix[k][i])\n\t\t\t\t\tstd::cout << i << j << k << std::endl;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid countTriangles(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (v <= u) continue;\n\t\t\tfor (int w : adj[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tif (w <= v) continue;\n\t\t\t\tfor (int target : adj[w]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif (target == u) /* Found triangle */;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nAdjacency matrix triangle counting is . Using an adjacency list reduces this to , which is much faster for graphs where the maximum degree is small relative to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid multiSourceBFS(int n, std::vector<int> sources, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int s : sources) {\n\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(s);\n\t\t// run individual BFS\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid combinedBFS(int n, const std::vector<int>& sources, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tfor (int s : sources) {\n\t\tdist[s] = 0; q.push(s);\n\t}\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nRunning separate BFS for each source is . A combined multi-source BFS processes each node and edge exactly once, reducing complexity to  regardless of the number of source nodes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSelfLoops(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint loops = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (i == j && matrix[i][j]) loops++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn loops;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSelfLoopsFast(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint loops = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[i][i]) loops++;\n\t}\n\treturn loops;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses a nested loop  to check the diagonal. The optimized version only iterates over the diagonal once, reducing time complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topologicalSortNaive(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\t// Brute force checking all permutations\n\t// O(N!)\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nstd::vector<int> kahnAlgo(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> in_degree(n, 0);\n\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) in_degree[v]++;\n\t}\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (in_degree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n\tstd::vector<int> order;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\torder.push_back(u);\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (--in_degree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn order;\n}\n---\nTopological sorting via Kahn's algorithm or DFS reduces the complexity from exponential (all permutations) to , providing an efficient way to schedule dependent tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isDirected(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[i][j] != matrix[j][i]) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isDirectedFast(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (matrix[i][j] != matrix[j][i]) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code checks every pair  and  separately, doubling the work. By only checking the upper triangle against the lower triangle (), we reduce the comparisons by half while maintaining ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countPaths(int u, int v, int matrix[100][100], int steps) {\n\tif (steps == 0) return u == v;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][i]) count += countPaths(i, v, matrix, steps - 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nusing Matrix = std::vector<std::vector<int>>;\n\nMatrix multiply(const Matrix& A, const Matrix& B) {\n\tint n = A.size();\n\tMatrix C(n, std::vector<int>(n, 0));\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tC[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];\n\treturn C;\n}\n---\nCounting paths of length  via recursion is . Using matrix exponentiation reduces this to  by exploiting the property that the -th entry of  is the number of paths of length  from  to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findBridges(int n, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> edges) {\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\t// remove e, check connectivity, add e\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid dfsBridges(int u, int p, int& timer, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, \n\t\t\t\tstd::vector<int>& tin, std::vector<int>& low) {\n\ttin[u] = low[u] = timer++;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (v == p) continue;\n\t\tif (tin[v] != -1) low[u] = std::min(low[u], tin[v]);\n\t\telse {\n\t\t\tdfsBridges(v, u, timer, adj, tin, low);\n\t\t\tlow[u] = std::min(low[u], low[v]);\n\t\t\tif (low[v] > tin[u]) /* u-v is bridge */;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBrute force bridge detection takes . Tarjan's or Tarjan-like algorithms use a single DFS to find all bridges in  by tracking entry and low-link times."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint primNaive(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::vector<int> key(n, INT_MAX);\n\tstd::vector<bool> inMST(n, false);\n\tkey[0] = 0;\n\tfor (int count = 0; count < n; count++) {\n\t\tint u = -1;\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++)\n\t\t\tif (!inMST[v] && (u == -1 || key[v] < key[u])) u = v;\n\t\tinMST[u] = true;\n\t\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; v++)\n\t\t\tif (matrix[u][v] && !inMST[v] && matrix[u][v] < key[v])\n\t\t\t\tkey[v] = matrix[u][v];\n\t}\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint primOptimized(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<>> pq;\n\tstd::vector<int> key(n, 1e9); \n\tstd::vector<bool> inMST(n, false);\n\tpq.push({0, 0}); key[0] = 0;\n\twhile (!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = pq.top().second; pq.pop();\n\t\tif (inMST[u]) continue;\n\t\tinMST[u] = true;\n\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!inMST[edge.first] && edge.second < key[edge.first]) {\n\t\t\t\tkey[edge.first] = edge.second;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({key[edge.first], edge.first});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nThe  Prim's algorithm is inefficient for sparse graphs. Using an adjacency list and a priority queue reduces the complexity to , significantly improving performance on large, non-dense datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid getDegrees(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint d = 0;\n\t\tfor (auto& e : edges) if (e.first == i || e.second == i) d++;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> getDegreesFast(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<int> degrees(n, 0);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tdegrees[e.first]++;\n\t\tdegrees[e.second]++;\n\t}\n\treturn degrees;\n}\n---\nThe original code scans the entire edge list for every vertex, resulting in  time. By iterating once through the edges and incrementing degree counters, the complexity is reduced to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid bfsBrute(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::vector<int> q; q.push_back(0);\n\tsize_t head = 0;\n\twhile(head < q.size()) {\n\t\tint u = q[head++];\n\t\tfor(int v=0; v<n; v++) if(matrix[u][v]) q.push_back(v);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid bfsCorrect(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(0);\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tvisited[0] = true;\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tvisited[v] = true;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original code lacks cycle detection and uses a vector as a queue, which can lead to  pops. The optimized BFS uses a `std::queue` for  pops and a visited array to ensure  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool hasEdge(int u, int v, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> edges) {\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) if (e.first == u && e.second == v) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nstruct Hash { \n\tsize_t operator()(const std::pair<int, int>& p) const { return p.first ^ p.second; } \n};\n\nbool hasEdgeFast(int u, int v, const std::unordered_set<std::pair<int, int>, Hash>& edgeSet) {\n\treturn edgeSet.find({u, v}) != edgeSet.end();\n}\n---\nSearching for an edge in a linear vector takes  time. Using a hash-based `unordered_set` (or a matrix for very dense graphs) allows for  average time complexity for edge existence queries."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findPathNaive(int u, int v, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][i] && matrix[i][v]) /* Found path of length 2 */;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <bitset>\n\nvoid findPathOptimized(int u, int v, int n, const std::vector<std::bitset<100>>& adj) {\n\tstd::bitset<100> common = adj[u] & adj[v]; // For undirected graphs\n\tif (common.any()) /* Path exists */;\n}\n---\nManual iteration to find common neighbors takes . Using `std::bitset` allows the intersection to be computed using bitwise AND, which is  due to word-level parallelism on modern CPUs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid dfsRecursive(int u, int adj[100][100], bool visited[100]) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n\t\tif (adj[u][i]) dfsRecursive(i, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n\nvoid dfsIterative(int start, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int n) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tstd::stack<int> s; s.push(start);\n\twhile (!s.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = s.top(); s.pop();\n\t\tif (visited[u]) continue;\n\t\tvisited[u] = true;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) s.push(v);\n\t}\n}\n---\nDeep recursion can cause stack overflow on large graphs. An iterative DFS using `std::stack` with an adjacency list avoids stack limits and reduces the iteration overhead from  to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid allPairsShortestPath(int n, int matrix[100][100], int d[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\td[i][j] = matrix[i][j];\n\t// Missing K loop\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& d) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tif (d[i][k] != 1e9 && d[k][j] != 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\td[i][j] = std::min(d[i][j], d[i][k] + d[k][j]);\n}\n---\nThe original code fails to correctly update distances. The Floyd-Warshall algorithm must use the intermediate vertex  in the outermost loop to ensure  correctness for all-pairs shortest paths."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid removeVertex(int v, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tmatrix[v][i] = 0;\n\t\tmatrix[i][v] = 0;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid removeVertexAdj(int v, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tadj[v].clear();\n\tfor (auto& neighbors : adj) {\n\t\tneighbors.erase(std::remove(neighbors.begin(), neighbors.end(), v), neighbors.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nWhile clearing a row/column in a matrix is , it leaves the matrix size . In an adjacency list, removing a vertex effectively reduces the number of edges to scan, improving subsequent  traversals."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint diameterBrute(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint max_dist = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\t// calculate dist(i, j) with BFS\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_dist;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint bfs(int start, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int& farthestNode) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(start); dist[start] = 0;\n\tint maxD = 0; farthestNode = start;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(dist[u] > maxD) { maxD = dist[u]; farthestNode = u; }\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(dist[v] == -1) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v); }\n\t}\n\treturn maxD;\n}\n\nint treeDiameter(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint nodeA, nodeB;\n\tbfs(0, n, adj, nodeA);\n\treturn bfs(nodeA, n, adj, nodeB);\n}\n---\nFor trees, the diameter can be found in  using two BFS passes instead of the  brute force approach of running BFS from every single node."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint countCommonNeighbors(int u, int v, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int x : adj[u]) {\n\t\tfor (int y : adj[v]) {\n\t\t\tif (x == y) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint countCommonFast(int u, int v, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::sort(adj[u].begin(), adj[u].end());\n\tstd::sort(adj[v].begin(), adj[v].end());\n\tstd::vector<int> intersect;\n\tstd::set_intersection(adj[u].begin(), adj[u].end(), adj[v].begin(), adj[v].end(), \n\t\t\t\t\t\t  std::back_inserter(intersect));\n\treturn intersect.size();\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop is . By sorting the adjacency lists once and using a two-pointer intersection, the complexity drops to  per query."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findIslands(int grid[50][50]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < 50; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (grid[i][j] == 1) /* find component */;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfsIsland(int r, int c, int grid[50][50]) {\n\tif (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= 50 || c >= 50 || grid[r][c] == 0) return;\n\tgrid[r][c] = 0;\n\tdfsIsland(r+1, c, grid); dfsIsland(r-1, c, grid);\n\tdfsIsland(r, c+1, grid); dfsIsland(r, c-1, grid);\n}\n---\nThe nested loop just identifies start points. A DFS that marks visited cells (or flips them to 0) ensures each cell is visited a constant number of times, resulting in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid naiveDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& path) {\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tnaiveDFS(v, adj, path);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid optimizedDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited, std::vector<int>& path) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif(!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\toptimizedDFS(v, adj, visited, path);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original recursive DFS lacks a visited check, leading to infinite loops in cyclic graphs or exponential redundant work O(2^N). Adding a visited array ensures each vertex and edge is processed exactly once, resulting in O(V + E) complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortestPath(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::vector<int>> q;\n\tq.push({start});\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> path = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tint last = path.back();\n\t\tif(last == end) return path.size() - 1;\n\t\tfor(int neighbor : adj[last]) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> next_path = path;\n\t\t\tnext_path.push_back(neighbor);\n\t\t\tq.push(next_path);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint findShortestPath(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tq.push(start);\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach stores full paths in the queue and lacks visited tracking, causing exponential memory growth. A standard BFS with a distance array finds the shortest path in an unweighted graph in O(V + E) time and O(V) space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isCyclicBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i < (1 << n); ++i) {\n\t\t// Highly inefficient brute force logic checking subsets\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent;\n\tDSU(int n) {\n\t\tparent.resize(n);\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) parent[i] = i;\n\t}\n\tint find(int i) {\n\t\tif(parent[i] == i) return i;\n\t\treturn parent[i] = find(parent[i]);\n\t}\n\tbool unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint root_i = find(i);\n\t\tint root_j = find(j);\n\t\tif(root_i != root_j) {\n\t\t\tparent[root_i] = root_j;\n\t\t\treturn true;\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor(auto& edge : edges) {\n\t\tif(!dsu.unite(edge.first, edge.second)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nBrute force cycle detection is computationally infeasible for large graphs. Using a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression allows cycle detection in nearly O(E) time, which is vastly superior to exponential or high-order polynomial search."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint countComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=i+1; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t// logic checking if path exists between every pair\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(!visited[v]) dfs(v, adj, visited);\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tif(!visited[i]) {\n\t\t\tcount++;\n\t\t\tdfs(i, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach of checking path existence for all pairs is O(V^2 * (V+E)). Iterating through nodes and triggering a DFS for unvisited nodes identifies all connected components in O(V + E) time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nconst int INF = 1e9;\nvoid allPairsShortestPath(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\t// Assume some naive repeated DFS for every node pair\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nconst int INF = 1e9;\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor(int k=0; k<n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif(dist[i][k] != INF && dist[k][j] != INF)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nRepeatedly running simpler algorithms for all pairs is often inefficient. Floyd-Warshall provides a structured O(V^3) approach to find all-pairs shortest paths, utilizing dynamic programming and offering better cache locality than fragmented searches."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Node { int val; std::vector<Node*> neighbors; };\nvoid traverse(Node* n) {\n\tif(!n) return;\n\tstd::cout << n->val << std::endl;\n\tfor(auto* next : n->neighbors) traverse(next);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nstruct Node { int val; std::vector<Node*> neighbors; };\nvoid traverse(Node* n, std::unordered_set<Node*>& visited) {\n\tif(!n || visited.count(n)) return;\n\tvisited.insert(n);\n\tstd::cout << n->val << std::endl;\n\tfor(auto* next : n->neighbors) traverse(next, visited);\n}\n---\nThe original traversal will enter an infinite loop if the graph contains any cycle. Adding a hash-set based visited check ensures O(V + E) complexity and prevents stack overflow from infinite recursion."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint dijkstraNaive(int n, int start, int target, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tint u = -1;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) if(!visited[j] && (u == -1 || dist[j] < dist[u])) u = j;\n\t\tvisited[u] = true;\n\t\tfor(int v=0; v<n; ++v) if(adj[u][v] != 0) dist[v] = std::min(dist[v], dist[u] + adj[u][v]);\n\t}\n\treturn dist[target];\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <climits>\n\ntypedef std::pair<int, int> pii;\nint dijkstraOptimized(int n, int start, int target, const std::vector<std::vector<pii>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tstd::priority_queue<pii, std::vector<pii>, std::greater<pii>> pq;\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tpq.push({0, start});\n\twhile(!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint d = pq.top().first, u = pq.top().second; pq.pop();\n\t\tif(d > dist[u]) continue;\n\t\tfor(auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tint v = edge.first, w = edge.second;\n\t\t\tif(dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({dist[v], v});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[target];\n}\n---\nThe naive Dijkstra uses O(V^2) complexity, which is slow for sparse graphs. Using an adjacency list with a priority queue (Min-Heap) reduces the complexity to O(E log V), significantly speeding up pathfinding in large networks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nbool hasPath(int u, int target, int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tif(u == target) return true;\n\tfor(int v=0; v<n; ++v) {\n\t\tif(matrix[u][v]) if(hasPath(v, target, n, matrix)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool hasPath(int start, int target, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start); visited[start] = true;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == target) return true;\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tvisited[v] = true;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe naive recursion lacks a visited check and uses an adjacency matrix, leading to O(V^V) in the worst case. The optimized BFS uses an adjacency list and visited array to ensure O(V + E) time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\nint findMST(int n, std::vector<Edge> edges) {\n\tstd::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](Edge a, Edge b){ return a.w < b.w; });\n\t// Naive cycle detection using DFS/BFS for every edge addition\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> p;\n\tDSU(int n) { p.resize(n); for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) p[i]=i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return p[i] == i ? i : (p[i] = find(p[i])); }\n\tbool unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint root_i = find(i), root_j = find(j);\n\t\tif(root_i != root_j) { p[root_i] = root_j; return true; }\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n};\n\nint kruskal(int n, std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tstd::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](Edge a, Edge b){ return a.w < b.w; });\n\tDSU dsu(n); int cost = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor(auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif(dsu.unite(e.u, e.v)) { cost += e.w; count++; }\n\t}\n\treturn cost;\n}\n---\nKruskal's algorithm with naive cycle detection is O(E * V). Using a DSU with path compression optimizes cycle detection to nearly O(1) per edge, bringing the total complexity down to O(E log E) due to sorting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint findRoot(int i, const std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\twhile(parent[i] != i) i = parent[i];\n\treturn i;\n}\n\nvoid naiveUnion(int i, int j, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tint root_i = findRoot(i, parent);\n\tint root_j = findRoot(j, parent);\n\tif(root_i != root_j) parent[root_i] = root_j;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint findOptimized(int i, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tif(parent[i] == i) return i;\n\treturn parent[i] = findOptimized(parent[i], parent);\n}\n\nvoid unionOptimized(int i, int j, std::vector<int>& parent, std::vector<int>& rank) {\n\tint r1 = findOptimized(i, parent);\n\tint r2 = findOptimized(j, parent);\n\tif(r1 != r2) {\n\t\tif(rank[r1] < rank[r2]) parent[r1] = r2;\n\t\telse if(rank[r1] > rank[r2]) parent[r2] = r1;\n\t\telse { parent[r1] = r2; rank[r2]++; }\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe naive DSU can form long chains, leading to O(N) operations. Path compression and union-by-rank optimize this to nearly O(alpha(N)), effectively constant time for any practical input size."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool isBipartite(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor(int start=0; start<n; ++start) {\n\t\tif(color[start] == -1) {\n\t\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(start); color[start] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\t\tfor(int v=0; v<n; ++v) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif(adj[u][v]) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tif(color[v] == -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.push(v); }\n\t\t\t\t\t\telse if(color[v] == color[u]) return false;\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool isBipartite(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tif(color[i] == -1) {\n\t\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(i); color[i] = 0;\n\t\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\t\tif(color[v] == -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.push(v); }\n\t\t\t\t\telse if(color[v] == color[u]) return false;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nThe adjacency matrix makes bipartite checking O(V^2). Switching to an adjacency list reduces the complexity to O(V + E), which is significantly more efficient for sparse graphs common in networking and social data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint getInDegree(int node, int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) if(matrix[i][node]) count++;\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> computeInDegrees(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> inDegree(n, 0);\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; ++u) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) inDegree[v]++;\n\t}\n\treturn inDegree;\n}\n---\nCalculating in-degree for a single node in a matrix is O(V). If done for all nodes, it becomes O(V^2). Using an adjacency list and a single pass allows pre-calculating all in-degrees in O(V + E) time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n\nvoid topoSort(int u, int n, int matrix[100][100], bool vis[], std::stack<int>& s) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor(int v=0; v<n; ++v) {\n\t\tif(matrix[u][v] && !vis[v]) topoSort(v, n, matrix, vis, s);\n\t}\n\ts.push(u);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nstd::vector<int> kahnTopoSort(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> inDegree(n, 0), result;\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; ++u) for(int v : adj[u]) inDegree[v]++;\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) if(inDegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop(); result.push_back(u);\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(--inDegree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nKahn's algorithm using an adjacency list is O(V + E). The recursive matrix-based DFS approach is O(V^2) and risks stack overflow on deep DAGs, whereas Kahn's is iterative and more cache-friendly."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid bellmanFord(int n, int src, const std::vector<std::pair<std::pair<int, int>, int>>& edges) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tdist[src] = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n-1; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(auto& e : edges) {\n\t\t\tint u = e.first.first, v = e.first.second, w = e.second;\n\t\t\tif(dist[u] != INT_MAX && dist[u] + w < dist[v]) dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid spfa(int n, int src, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tstd::vector<bool> inQueue(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tdist[src] = 0; q.push(src); inQueue[src] = true;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop(); inQueue[u] = false;\n\t\tfor(auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tint v = edge.first, w = edge.second;\n\t\t\tif(dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n\t\t\t\tif(!inQueue[v]) { q.push(v); inQueue[v] = true; }\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nWhile Bellman-Ford is O(V * E), the Shortest Path Faster Algorithm (SPFA) often performs in O(k * E) where k is a small constant. This optimization avoids redundant edge relaxations by only processing nodes whose distances have actually changed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isReach(int u, int target, std::vector<int> adj[], bool vis[]) {\n\tif(u == target) return true;\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(!vis[v] && isReach(v, target, adj, vis)) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool isReachIterative(int start, int target, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start); vis[start] = true;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == target) return true;\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(!vis[v]) { vis[v] = true; q.push(v); }\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nDeeply nested recursive DFS can cause O(V) stack depth, risking stack overflow. An iterative BFS uses O(V + E) time but manages memory on the heap (queue), which is much safer and typically faster for reachability in large graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v; };\nstd::vector<Edge> findAllEdges(int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tstd::vector<Edge> edges;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) edges.push_back({i, j});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn edges;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v; };\nstd::vector<Edge> findAllEdges(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<Edge> edges;\n\tfor(int u=0; u < (int)adj.size(); ++u) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) edges.push_back({u, v});\n\t}\n\treturn edges;\n}\n---\nIterating over an adjacency matrix takes O(V^2) regardless of edge density. The adjacency list approach completes in O(V + E), providing a significant speedup for the sparse graphs typically found in real-world scenarios."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid findTransitiveClosure(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, bool reach[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t// Some O(V+E) search for every pair\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <bitset>\n\nvoid transitiveClosure(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<std::bitset<100>>& reach) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) reach[i].set(i);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) for(int v : adj[i]) reach[i].set(v);\n\tfor(int k=0; k<n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tif(reach[i].test(k)) reach[i] |= reach[k];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nStandard transitive closure via repeated DFS is O(V^2 * (V+E)). Using Floyd-Warshall with `std::bitset` optimizations reduces the complexity by a word-size factor (typically 64), resulting in O(V^3 / 64) and excellent cache performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countEdges(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i)\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j)\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) count++;\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countEdges(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(auto& neighbors : adj) count += neighbors.size();\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe matrix-based edge counting is always O(V^2). With an adjacency list, the operation becomes O(V), simply summing the sizes of the adjacency vectors, which is much faster for graphs where E << V^2."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasPathBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges, int u, int v) {\n\t// Assume a function that recursively checks all permutations\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> p;\n\tDSU(int n) { p.resize(n); for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) p[i]=i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return p[i] == i ? i : (p[i] = find(p[i])); }\n\tvoid unite(int i, int j) { p[find(i)] = find(j); }\n};\n\nbool isConnected(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges, int u, int v) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor(auto& e : edges) dsu.unite(e.first, e.second);\n\treturn dsu.find(u) == dsu.find(v);\n}\n---\nBrute-force path checking is exponential. Using DSU for static connectivity queries takes O(E * alpha(V)) to build and O(alpha(V)) to query, which is essentially constant time compared to traversing the graph for every query."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findCycle(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int> path) {\n\tfor(int node : path) if(node == u) { /* cycle found */ }\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) findCycle(v, adj, path);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasCycle(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1; // Visiting\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif(state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif(state[v] == 0 && hasCycle(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2; // Visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code checks the entire path for every node, leading to O(V^2) or worse. Using 3-color DFS (unvisited, visiting, visited) allows cycle detection in a single O(V + E) pass by identifying back-edges."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid getTranspose(int n, int matrix[500][500], int result[500][500]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tresult[j][i] = matrix[i][j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> getTranspose(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> trans(n);\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; ++u) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) trans[v].push_back(u);\n\t}\n\treturn trans;\n}\n---\nTransposing a matrix is always O(V^2). For sparse graphs, transposing an adjacency list takes O(V + E) time and proportional space, which is far more efficient when the number of edges is small relative to V^2."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint findMinEdge(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint minWeight = INT_MAX;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i)\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j)\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j] > 0 && matrix[i][j] < minWeight) minWeight = matrix[i][j];\n\treturn minWeight;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\nint findMinEdge(const std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tint minWeight = INT_MAX;\n\tfor(const auto& e : edges) if(e.w < minWeight) minWeight = e.w;\n\treturn minWeight;\n}\n---\nSearching for the minimum weight in an adjacency matrix is O(V^2). Maintaining an edge list allows for an O(E) search, which is significantly faster in graphs where the number of edges is much smaller than the square of the vertex count."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findBridges(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) {\n\t\t\t\t// Remove edge, check connectivity with DFS, add edge back\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid bridgeDFS(int u, int p, int& timer, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, \n\t\t\t   std::vector<int>& tin, std::vector<int>& low) {\n\ttin[u] = low[u] = timer++;\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif(v == p) continue;\n\t\tif(tin[v] != -1) low[u] = std::min(low[u], tin[v]);\n\t\telse {\n\t\t\tbridgeDFS(v, u, timer, adj, tin, low);\n\t\t\tlow[u] = std::min(low[u], low[v]);\n\t\t\tif(low[v] > tin[u]) { /* Bridge found: u-v */ }\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBrute force bridge detection is O(E * (V + E)). Tarjan's or Kosaraju's bridge-finding algorithms use discovery and low-link times to find all bridges in a single O(V + E) pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findUniversalSink(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tbool isSink = true;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif(i == j) continue;\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j] || !matrix[j][i]) { isSink = false; break; }\n\t\t}\n\t\tif(isSink) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint findUniversalSink(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint i = 0, j = 0;\n\twhile(i < n && j < n) {\n\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) i++;\n\t\telse j++;\n\t}\n\tif(i == n) return -1;\n\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++) {\n\t\tif(i == k) continue;\n\t\tif(matrix[i][k] || !matrix[k][i]) return -1;\n\t}\n\treturn i;\n}\n---\nThe naive universal sink search is O(V^2). A refined elimination approach uses the matrix properties to narrow down the candidate in O(V) time, followed by a single O(V) verification pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isComplete(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tif(adj[i].size() != n - 1) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isComplete(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tlong long expected = (long long)n * (n - 1);\n\tlong long actual = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) actual += adj[i].size();\n\treturn actual == expected;\n}\n---\nWhile both are O(V), the second version avoids early exits only if necessary and summarizes the check efficiently. In many contexts, checking edge count O(1) against  is even faster if the count is pre-maintained."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid findAllDistances(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Run a full BFS from every single node\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++)\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tif(dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j])\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];\n}\n---\nRunning BFS O(V) times is O(V(V+E)). While O(V^3) Floyd-Warshall is theoretically slower for very sparse graphs, its tight inner loop and better cache locality make it highly efficient for all-pairs distances in dense graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasIsolatedNode(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tbool connected = false;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j] || matrix[j][i]) { connected = true; break; }\n\t\t}\n\t\tif(!connected) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasIsolatedNode(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> degree(n, 0);\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; u++) {\n\t\tdegree[u] += adj[u].size();\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) degree[v]++;\n\t}\n\tfor(int d : degree) if(d == 0) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe matrix approach is O(V^2). By using an adjacency list and calculating degrees in O(V + E), we can identify isolated nodes much faster in sparse graphs where the number of edges is small."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid findPath(int u, int v, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& path) {\n\tpath.push_back(u);\n\tif(u == v) return;\n\tfor(int neighbor : adj[u]) findPath(neighbor, v, adj, path);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> findPathBFS(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start); parent[start] = start;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == end) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> path;\n\t\t\tfor(int curr = end; curr != start; curr = parent[curr]) path.push_back(curr);\n\t\t\tpath.push_back(start); std::reverse(path.begin(), path.end());\n\t\t\treturn path;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(parent[v] == -1) { parent[v] = u; q.push(v); }\n\t}\n\treturn {};\n}\n---\nRecursive pathfinding without visited tracking is O(Branch^Depth). BFS with a parent array ensures O(V + E) complexity and guarantees finding the shortest path in terms of number of edges."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid findSCCs(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\t// Check if i can reach j AND j can reach i using naive BFS\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis, std::stack<int>& s) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis, s);\n\ts.push(u);\n}\n\nvoid findSCCs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::stack<int> s; std::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(!vis[i]) dfs(i, adj, vis, s);\n\t// Transpose and second DFS omitted for brevity, logic is O(V+E)\n}\n---\nChecking reachability for all pairs to find SCCs is O(V^2 * (V+E)). Tarjan's or Kosaraju's algorithm finds all Strongly Connected Components in O(V + E) time using discovery times or graph transposition."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isSelfLoop(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif(i == j && matrix[i][j]) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isSelfLoop(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(matrix[i][i]) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code performs an unnecessary nested loop, resulting in O(V^2) complexity. Since self-loops only occur on the main diagonal of the adjacency matrix, we only need to check O(V) elements."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint countTriangles(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++)\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++)\n\t\t\tfor(int k=0; k<n; k++)\n\t\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j] && matrix[j][k] && matrix[k][i]) count++;\n\treturn count / 6;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint countTriangles(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; u++) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(v <= u) continue;\n\t\t\tfor(int w : adj[v]) {\n\t\t\t\tif(w <= v) continue;\n\t\t\t\tfor(int check : adj[w]) if(check == u) count++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe matrix approach is O(V^3). For sparse graphs, iterating over adjacency lists and using vertex ordering to avoid overcounting reduces the complexity toward O(E^1.5), significantly improving performance on real-world datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid removeEdge(int u, int v, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tmatrix[u][v] = 0;\n\tmatrix[v][u] = 0;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid removeEdge(int u, int v, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tadj[u].erase(std::remove(adj[u].begin(), adj[u].end(), v), adj[u].end());\n\tadj[v].erase(std::remove(adj[v].begin(), adj[v].end(), u), adj[v].end());\n}\n---\nWhile matrix removal is O(1), an adjacency list removal is O(degree(u)). However, the adjacency list is vastly more efficient for subsequent graph traversals, making it the preferred choice for sparse graphs despite the slight overhead in edge deletion."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isTournament(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=i+1; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif(!((matrix[i][j] || matrix[j][i]) && !(matrix[i][j] && matrix[j][i]))) return false;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isTournament(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint edges = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tedges += adj[i].size();\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[i]) if(i == v) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn edges == n * (n - 1) / 2;\n}\n---\nThe matrix check is O(V^2). While a tournament graph is dense (E is O(V^2)), verifying the edge count and lack of self-loops or bidirectionality in an adjacency list is O(V + E), which remains efficient."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint maxFlowNaive(int s, int t, int cap[100][100]) {\n\t// Some extremely slow brute force path search\n\treturn 0;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint bfs(int s, int t, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& cap, std::vector<int>& parent) {\n\tstd::fill(parent.begin(), parent.end(), -1);\n\tparent[s] = -2;\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({s, 1e9});\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front().first, flow = q.front().second; q.pop();\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(parent[v] == -1 && cap[u][v] > 0) {\n\t\t\t\tparent[v] = u;\n\t\t\t\tint new_flow = std::min(flow, cap[u][v]);\n\t\t\t\tif(v == t) return new_flow;\n\t\t\t\tq.push({v, new_flow});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn 0;\n}\n---\nEdmonds-Karp implements the Ford-Fulkerson method using BFS to find augmenting paths, ensuring O(V * E^2) complexity. This is significantly faster than naive depth-based searches which can have exponential behavior depending on capacities."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid getDensity(int v, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint edges = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<v; i++) for(int j=0; j<v; j++) if(matrix[i][j]) edges++;\n\tstd::cout << (double)edges / (v * (v-1));\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid getDensity(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint v = adj.size();\n\tif(v <= 1) return;\n\tlong long edges = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<v; i++) edges += adj[i].size();\n\tstd::cout << (double)edges / (v * (v - 1));\n}\n---\nCalculating graph density with an adjacency matrix requires O(V^2) work. With an adjacency list, we only iterate over the nodes and sum their neighbor counts, resulting in O(V) time, which is much faster for large graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isRegular(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint k = -1;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tint degree = 0;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(matrix[i][j]) degree++;\n\t\tif(k == -1) k = degree;\n\t\telse if(k != degree) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isRegular(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tif(adj.empty()) return true;\n\tsize_t k = adj[0].size();\n\tfor(const auto& neighbors : adj) if(neighbors.size() != k) return false;\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nThe matrix-based regularity check is O(V^2). Using an adjacency list reduces the check to O(V) because the degree of each node is readily available as the size of the adjacency vector."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findPredecessors(int node, int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tif(matrix[i][node]) { /* i is a predecessor */ }\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findPredecessors(int node, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& rev_adj) {\n\tfor(int pred : rev_adj[node]) {\n\t\t/* pred is a predecessor */\n\t}\n}\n---\nFinding predecessors in a standard adjacency list or matrix is O(V). By maintaining a reversed adjacency list, this operation becomes O(in-degree(node)), which is significantly faster in sparse directed graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <cmath>\n\nvoid clusterNodes(int n, double x[], double y[]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\tdouble dist = std::sqrt(std::pow(x[i]-x[j], 2) + std::pow(y[i]-y[j], 2));\n\t\t\t// Brute force distance check\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <cmath>\n\nstruct Point { double x, y; };\n// Use a spatial data structure like a k-d tree\n// This is a placeholder for the concept of spatial partitioning\n---\nAll-pairs distance calculation is O(N^2). Spatial partitioning data structures like k-d trees or Quadtrees allow for nearest-neighbor and range searches in O(log N) or O(sqrt N), greatly accelerating clustering algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool isStronglyConnected(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\t// Run DFS from every node to see if it reaches every other node\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nbool isStronglyConnected(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tdfs(0, adj, vis);\n\tfor(bool v : vis) if(!v) return false;\n\t// Transpose and check again (Kosaraju's first step)\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nNaive strong connectivity checking is O(V(V+E)). Using Kosaraju's or Tarjan's algorithm reduces the check to O(V + E) by verifying reachability from a single node in both the original and transposed graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid printLevel(int start, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, level] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t// process and add neighbors from matrix\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nvoid printLevel(int start, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(adj.size(), -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tq.push(start); dist[start] = 0;\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original approach might re-process nodes without a visited check, leading to exponential complexity. Standard BFS with a distance array provides O(V + E) complexity and correctly tracks levels for each node."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findCenter(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\t// A center has degree n-1. Naive check:\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tint d = 0;\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(matrix[i][j]) d++;\n\t\tif(d == n-1) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint findCenter(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint n = adj.size();\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if((int)adj[i].size() == n - 1) return i;\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe matrix-based degree calculation is O(V^2). With an adjacency list, the degree of a node is O(1) to retrieve (the size of the vector), making the total search O(V)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nvoid getUniqueEdges(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tstd::set<std::pair<int, int>> edges;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) edges.insert({std::min(i,j), std::max(i,j)});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v; };\nstd::vector<Edge> getUniqueEdges(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<Edge> result;\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; u++) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) if(u < v) result.push_back({u, v});\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nUsing a set to store unique edges from a matrix takes O(V^2 log E). By iterating over an adjacency list and applying a simple `u < v` constraint, we extract unique edges in O(V + E) time without set overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasOddCycle(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\t// Use brute force to find all cycles and check lengths\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool hasOddCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> color(n, -1);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) {\n\t\tif(color[i] != -1) continue;\n\t\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(i); color[i] = 0;\n\t\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\tif(color[v] == -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.push(v); }\n\t\t\t\telse if(color[v] == color[u]) return true;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nA graph has an odd cycle if and only if it is not bipartite. Bipartite checking using BFS/DFS is O(V + E), which is exponentially faster than brute-force cycle enumeration."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint getShortestEdge(int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tint minW = INT_MAX;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) for(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(matrix[i][j] && matrix[i][j] < minW) minW = matrix[i][j];\n\treturn minW;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nstruct Edge { int u, v, w; };\nint getShortestEdge(const std::vector<Edge>& edges) {\n\tint minW = INT_MAX;\n\tfor(const auto& e : edges) if(e.w < minW) minW = e.w;\n\treturn minW;\n}\n---\nThe matrix approach is always O(V^2). For sparse graphs, maintaining an explicit list of edges allows finding the minimum weight in O(E) time, which is significantly faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortAdjacencyList(std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor(auto& list : adj) {\n\t\tfor(size_t i=0; i<list.size(); i++) {\n\t\t\tfor(size_t j=i+1; j<list.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\t\tif(list[i] > list[j]) std::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortAdjacencyList(std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor(auto& list : adj) {\n\t\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual bubble sort of adjacency lists is O(V * degree^2). Using `std::sort` (IntroSort) provides O(E log(max_degree)) complexity, making the graph normalization much faster for nodes with high degrees."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isSource(int u, int n, int matrix[100][100]) {\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(matrix[i][u]) return false;\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<bool> findSources(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> isSource(n, true);\n\tfor(int u=0; u<n; u++) {\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) isSource[v] = false;\n\t}\n\treturn isSource;\n}\n---\nChecking if a single node is a source in a matrix is O(V). Finding all sources by repeating this is O(V^2). An adjacency list allows finding all sources in O(V + E) time by tracking in-degrees."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSelfLoops(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) for(int j=0; j<n; j++) if(i == j && matrix[i][j]) count++;\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSelfLoops(int n, int matrix[1000][1000]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(matrix[i][i]) count++;\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original O(V^2) approach scans every pair. Since self-loops only occur on the diagonal where i == j, a single O(V) pass through the diagonal is sufficient and much faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid traverse(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\t// Potential infinite recursion on cycles\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\ttraverse(neighbor, adj);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid traverse(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[neighbor]) {\n\t\t\ttraverse(neighbor, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original code lacks a visited check, leading to exponential redundant work O(branching_factor^depth) or infinite loops in cyclic graphs. Adding a bitset or vector of booleans ensures each node is visited exactly once, resulting in O(V + E) complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint findShortestDistance(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::pair<int, int> curr = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(curr.first == end) return curr.second;\n\t\tfor(int neighbor : adj[curr.first]) {\n\t\t\tq.push({neighbor, curr.second + 1});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint findShortestDistance(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tdist[start] = 0; q.push(start);\n\twhile(!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif(u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor(int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif(dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code allows nodes to be added to the queue multiple times, leading to O(2^V) path exploration. Implementing a standard BFS with a distance/visited array ensures O(V + E) time by visiting each vertex and edge at most once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool hasCycleBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tfor(auto const& edge : edges) {\n\t\t// Highly inefficient logic checking every possible path back\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent;\n\tDSU(int n) : parent(n) { for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) parent[i] = i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return (parent[i] == i) ? i : (parent[i] = find(parent[i])); }\n\tbool unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint root_i = find(i), root_j = find(j);\n\t\tif(root_i != root_j) { parent[root_i] = root_j; return true; }\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor(auto const& edge : edges) {\n\t\tif(!dsu.unite(edge.first, edge.second)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nDetecting cycles in an undirected graph via brute force is computationally expensive. Using a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression provides a near O(E α(V)) solution, where α is the inverse Ackermann function, making it essentially linear for all practical purposes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint countConnectedComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tint components = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Naive nested loops checking path existence via brute force\n\t}\n\treturn components;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) dfs(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n\nint countConnectedComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint components = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!visited[i]) {\n\t\t\tcomponents++;\n\t\t\tdfs(i, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn components;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach of checking path existence between every pair of nodes results in O(V^3) or worse complexity. By using DFS to mark all reachable nodes from an unvisited source, we can count components in O(V + E) time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid allPairsShortestPath(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t// Brute force: perform a full BFS/Dijkstra inside here\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nconst int INF = 1e9;\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] < INF && dist[k][j] < INF)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nRunning naive pathfinding between every pair results in highly redundant computations and poor cache performance. Floyd-Warshall uses dynamic programming to solve the all-pairs shortest path problem in O(V^3) time with very low overhead and excellent data locality."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nclass DenseGraph {\n\tbool matrix[5000][5000];\npublic:\n\tDenseGraph() { /* init to false */ }\n\tvoid addEdge(int u, int v) { matrix[u][v] = true; }\n\tint countEdges(int v_count) {\n\t\tint count = 0;\n\t\tfor(int i=0; i<v_count; ++i)\n\t\t\tfor(int j=0; j<v_count; ++j)\n\t\t\t\tif(matrix[i][j]) count++;\n\t\treturn count;\n\t}\n};", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nclass DenseGraph {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adj;\n\tint edgeCount = 0;\npublic:\n\tDenseGraph(int n) : adj(n) {}\n\tvoid addEdge(int u, int v) { adj[u].push_back(v); edgeCount++; }\n\tint countEdges() {\n\t\treturn edgeCount;\n\t}\n};\n---\nThe adjacency matrix approach requires O(V^2) time to iterate over edges, which is inefficient for most graph types. Storing an explicit count or using an adjacency list reduces this to O(1) for counting or O(V+E) for iteration, providing massive speedups for large vertex counts."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint naiveShortest(int n, int start, int target, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tdist[start] = 0;\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int u = 0; u < n; ++u) {\n\t\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[u] != INT_MAX && dist[u] + edge.second < dist[edge.first])\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[edge.first] = dist[u] + edge.second;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[target];\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <climits>\n\nint dijkstra(int n, int start, int target, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);\n\tstd::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<std::pair<int, int>>> pq;\n\tdist[start] = 0; pq.push({0, start});\n\twhile (!pq.empty()) {\n\t\tint d = pq.top().first, u = pq.top().second; pq.pop();\n\t\tif (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\t\tfor (auto& edge : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[u] + edge.second < dist[edge.first]) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[edge.first] = dist[u] + edge.second;\n\t\t\t\tpq.push({dist[edge.first], edge.first});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn dist[target];\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized Bellman-Ford style approach takes O(V * E) regardless of the graph structure. Dijkstra's algorithm with a priority queue reduces complexity to O(E log V), which is significantly faster for non-negative edge weights on large graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nbool hasCycle(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::unordered_set<int> path) {\n\tif (path.count(u)) return true;\n\tpath.insert(u);\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (hasCycle(v, adj, path)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasCycleDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1; // Visiting\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && hasCycleDFS(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2; // Visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code copies a hash set at every recursion level, leading to O(V^2) space and O(V^2) or worse time due to hashing overhead. Using a 3-state integer vector (0: unvisited, 1: visiting, 2: visited) allows O(V + E) cycle detection in a single pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> checked(n, false);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0; j<n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t// Brute force check if i and j are connected\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid explore(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) explore(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!visited[i]) {\n\t\t\tcount++;\n\t\t\texplore(i, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized logic performs exhaustive path checks for every vertex pair, leading to O(V^2 * (V+E)) time. Using a single DFS pass to mark all vertices in a component reduces the complexity to O(V + E)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAllShortest(int n, int matrix[200][200]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t// Brute force BFS from every node i\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] != 1e9 && dist[k][j] != 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nRunning separate BFS/Dijkstra searches from every vertex often results in high constant factors and poor use of the memory hierarchy. Floyd-Warshall provides a compact O(V^3) solution that is highly cache-efficient and simple to implement for dense graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfsRecursive(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\t// No visited array, just branching\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\tdfsRecursive(neighbor, adj);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfsRecursive(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) {\n\t\t\tdfsRecursive(v, adj, visited);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nWithout tracking visited nodes, DFS becomes a tree traversal that visits the same node multiple times across different paths, leading to O(k^d) complexity. Using a visited array ensures each node is processed once, resulting in linear O(V + E) time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint getShortestPath(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::vector<int>> q;\n\tq.push({start});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> path = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tint u = path.back();\n\t\tif (u == end) return path.size() - 1;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> next_path = path;\n\t\t\tnext_path.push_back(v);\n\t\t\tq.push(next_path);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint getShortestPath(int start, int end, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tstd::queue<int> q;\n\tdist[start] = 0; q.push(start);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe original code stores full paths and lacks visited tracking, resulting in O(V!) potential paths and heavy memory allocation. BFS with a distance array reduces the complexity to O(V + E) and minimizes memory overhead per node."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool bruteCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Use deep DFS to check if node i can reach itself via back-path\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool dfsCycle(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& color) {\n\tcolor[u] = 1;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (color[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (color[v] == 0 && dfsCycle(v, adj, color)) return true;\n\t}\n\tcolor[u] = 2;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized logic performs redundant DFS searches from every node. By using 3-color DFS (0=unvisited, 1=visiting, 2=finished), we can detect cycles in a single O(V + E) pass by identifying back-edges in the DFS tree."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint countClusters(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tint clusters = 0;\n\tstd::vector<bool> seen(n, false);\n\tfor(int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Brute force check connectivity to existing clusters\n\t}\n\treturn clusters;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct UnionFind {\n\tstd::vector<int> p;\n\tUnionFind(int n) : p(n) { for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) p[i]=i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return (p[i] == i) ? i : (p[i] = find(p[i])); }\n\tvoid unite(int i, int j) { i = find(i); j = find(j); if(i != j) p[i] = j; }\n};\n\nint countClusters(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tUnionFind uf(n);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) uf.unite(e.first, e.second);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) if (uf.p[i] == i) count++;\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nBrute force connectivity checking is O(V^2) or worse. Union-Find with path compression allows merging and counting connected components in O(E α(V)) time, which is highly efficient for dynamic graph data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAPSP(int n, int adj[300][300]) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> res(n, std::vector<int>(n, INT_MAX));\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Run Dijkstra/BFS from node i\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] != 1e9 && dist[k][j] != 1e9) {\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nRepeatedly running single-source algorithms often incurs overhead in initialization and data management. Floyd-Warshall provides an O(V^3) solution with a very tight inner loop, which is significantly faster in practice for small to medium dense graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid recursiveSearch(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\t// No cycle protection or visited tracking\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\trecursiveSearch(v, adj);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid searchDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) searchDFS(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs exponential work by re-visiting nodes via different paths. Adding a simple visited array ensures each node and edge is processed at most once, providing a linear O(V + E) time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint naiveShortest(int n, int start, int goal, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, d] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == goal) return d;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) q.push({v, d + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint optimizedBFS(int n, int start, int goal, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[start] = 0; \n\tstd::queue<int> q; q.push(start);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == goal) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n\t\t\t\tq.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized BFS allows redundant node entries in the queue, leading to O(B^D) complexity. Proper BFS with a visited/distance check ensures each node is enqueued once, reducing time to O(V + E)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nbool hasCycleNaive(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Brute force: find all paths from i to i\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isCyclic(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& color) {\n\tcolor[u] = 1; // Visiting\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (color[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (color[v] == 0 && isCyclic(v, adj, color)) return true;\n\t}\n\tcolor[u] = 2; // Visited\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nBrute force cycle detection is extremely inefficient for large graphs. DFS with recursion states (color/state array) identifies back-edges in O(V + E) time, which is the optimal complexity for cycle detection in directed graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findNumComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Expensive connectivity checks\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!vis[i]) { count++; dfs(i, adj, vis); }\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original code lacks a systematic way to explore components, leading to redundant work. Using DFS to traverse each component once ensures O(V + E) total time, which is significantly faster than any O(V^2) pairwise approach."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid computeAllPairs(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> dists(n, std::vector<int>(n, INT_MAX));\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Naive path search\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& d) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif (d[i][k] != 1e9 && d[k][j] != 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\td[i][j] = std::min(d[i][j], d[i][k] + d[k][j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nExhaustive path search for every node is O(V^2 * E). Floyd-Warshall is O(V^3) and is much faster for dense graphs because of its simple structure and hardware-friendly access patterns."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid visit(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\t// Missing visited check\n\tfor (int neighbor : adj[u]) {\n\t\tvisit(neighbor, adj);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid visit(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (!visited[v]) visit(v, adj, visited);\n\t}\n}\n---\nRecursive DFS without visited tracking can loop forever on cycles and performs redundant work on DAGs. Adding a visited array makes it O(V + E) by ensuring each node is processed exactly once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint distance(int n, int s, int t, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({s, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, d] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return d;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) q.push({v, d + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint distance(int n, int s, int t, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[s] = 0; std::queue<int> q; q.push(s);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nWithout marking nodes as visited, BFS explorations become exponential as they re-add the same nodes to the queue. Introducing a distance array enables O(V + E) complexity by limiting each node to a single enqueue operation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool bruteForceCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\t// Implementation checking every path perm\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> p;\n\tDSU(int n) : p(n) { for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) p[i]=i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return p[i] == i ? i : (p[i] = find(p[i])); }\n\tbool unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint r1 = find(i), r2 = find(j);\n\t\tif (r1 != r2) { p[r1] = r2; return true; }\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) {\n\t\tif (!dsu.unite(e.first, e.second)) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nCycle detection via brute force pathfinding is O(V!). Using a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) structure with path compression allows us to detect cycles in nearly O(E) time for undirected graphs by checking for existing connectivity between endpoints."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countIslands(int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\t// Brute force: check for isolated groups via O(V^2) comparisons\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, int n, int matrix[500][500], std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v = 0; v < n; ++v) {\n\t\tif (matrix[u][v] && !vis[v]) dfs(v, n, matrix, vis);\n\t}\n}\n\nint countIslands(int n, int matrix[500][500]) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!vis[i]) { count++; dfs(i, n, matrix, vis); }\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original pairwise comparison approach is O(V^3). Using DFS/BFS to traverse and mark components reduces the complexity to O(V^2) (for matrix input), but switching to an adjacency list would further reduce it to O(V + E)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid allPairs(int n, int adj[100][100]) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t// Naive path search code\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] != 1e9 && dist[k][j] != 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nFor small dense graphs, Floyd-Warshall's O(V^3) complexity is far more efficient than naive all-pairs algorithms due to its extreme simplicity and cache-friendly nested loops, avoiding the constant overhead of complex data structures."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid visit(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) visit(v, adj);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid visit(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) visit(v, adj, visited);\n}\n---\nInfinite recursion and redundant path processing are major bottlenecks in graph traversals. Adding a visited array ensures each node is only explored once, dropping the time complexity from exponential O(B^D) to linear O(V + E)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint getDist(int n, int start, int end, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::pair<int, int> node = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (node.first == end) return node.second;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[node.first]) q.push({v, node.second + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint getDist(int n, int start, int end, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[start] = 0; std::queue<int> q; q.push(start);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nNodes are repeatedly added to the queue in the naive implementation, leading to exponential work. Using a distance array to prevent re-processing nodes ensures O(V + E) time, which is optimal for unweighted shortest paths."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool naiveCycleCheck(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\t// Uses repeated searches to find any loop\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> parent;\n\tDSU(int n) : parent(n) { for(int i=0; i<n; ++i) parent[i] = i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return (parent[i] == i) ? i : (parent[i] = find(parent[i])); }\n\tbool join(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint r1 = find(i), r2 = find(j);\n\t\tif (r1 == r2) return false;\n\t\tparent[r1] = r2; return true;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool isCyclic(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) if (!dsu.join(e.first, e.second)) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nCycle detection with brute-force path checking is O(V!). Union-Find with path compression provides near-linear O(E α(V)) time complexity by keeping track of connected components and detecting when an edge connects two already-connected nodes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSets(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\t// Brute force logic to find disjoint node groups\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid walk(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) walk(v, adj, visited);\n}\n\nint countSets(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!visited[i]) { count++; walk(i, adj, visited); }\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized approach of checking group membership between all pairs is O(V^3). Using DFS to traverse and mark components ensures we only visit each node and edge once, giving O(V + E) complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAPSP(int n, int m[400][400]) {\n\t// Manual nested loops checking all intermediates\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; ++k)\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j)\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] < 1e9 && dist[k][j] < 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n}\n---\nManual all-pairs path search is often O(V^4) or poorly structured O(V^3). Floyd-Warshall's O(V^3) implementation is significantly faster due to the SIMD-friendly nature of its nested loops and minimal branching inside the inner loop."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid deepDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) deepDFS(v, adj);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid properDFS(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) properDFS(v, adj, visited);\n}\n---\nRecursive traversals without visited checks result in redundant visits and potential infinite loops, causing exponential time complexity. Adding a visited array makes the traversal linear O(V + E) by restricting each node to a single exploration."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint findPath(int s, int t, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({s, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, d] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return d;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) q.push({v, d + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint findPath(int s, int t, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[s] = 0; std::queue<int> q; q.push(s);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe original code permits multiple entries of the same node in the queue, causing exponential path growth. Using a distance array to track visited states reduces the complexity to O(V + E) and ensures linear memory usage."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool isCyclicBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\t// Inefficiently search for any node reachable from itself\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DSU {\n\tstd::vector<int> par;\n\tDSU(int n) : par(n) { for(int i=0; i<n; i++) par[i]=i; }\n\tint find(int i) { return par[i] == i ? i : (par[i] = find(par[i])); }\n\tbool unite(int i, int j) {\n\t\tint r1 = find(i), r2 = find(j);\n\t\tif (r1 != r2) { par[r1] = r2; return true; }\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n};\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& edges) {\n\tDSU dsu(n);\n\tfor (auto& e : edges) if (!dsu.unite(e.first, e.second)) return true;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nBrute-force cycle search can be O(V!). Union-Find with path compression enables cycle detection in O(E α(V)) time by checking if the endpoints of a new edge already belong to the same connected component."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countAllComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint c = 0;\n\t// Naive path existence checks for every node pair\n\treturn c;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!vis[i]) { count++; dfs(i, adj, vis); }\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original approach is O(V^2 * (V+E)). By using a single DFS pass to discover and mark all nodes in each connected component, we reduce the complexity to linear O(V + E)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAPSP(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>>& adj) {\n\t// Repeatedly calling Bellman-Ford for every node\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] < 1e9 && dist[k][j] < 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n}\n---\nRepeating O(V * E) Bellman-Ford results in O(V^2 * E), which is slow. Floyd-Warshall's O(V^3) complexity is much faster for dense graphs and offers better cache efficiency than multiple discrete path searches."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) dfs(v, adj);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v, adj, visited);\n}\n---\nTraversing graphs without visited tracking is exponential because it treats the graph as a tree with infinite paths. Introducing a visited array ensures each node is processed once, resulting in linear O(V + E) complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint getShortest(int n, int start, int end, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;\n\tq.push({start, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, d] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return d;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) q.push({v, d + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint getShortest(int n, int start, int end, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[start] = 0; std::queue<int> q; q.push(start);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == end) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nRedundant enqueue operations lead to O(Branching^Depth) time. Using a distance array to prevent re-visiting nodes reduces the search to O(V + E) and provides a correct shortest path in linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool hasCycleBrute(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\t// Naive path perm searches for self-reachability\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nbool dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<int>& state) {\n\tstate[u] = 1;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\tif (state[v] == 1) return true;\n\t\tif (state[v] == 0 && dfs(v, adj, state)) return true;\n\t}\n\tstate[u] = 2;\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nBrute-force cycle checks are O(V!). 3-color DFS (unvisited, visiting, visited) identifies back-edges in O(V + E) time, making it the most efficient algorithm for cycle detection in directed graphs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countIslands(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\t// Naive connectivity test between all possible pairs\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& vis) {\n\tvis[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!vis[v]) dfs(v, adj, vis);\n}\n\nint countIslands(int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<bool> vis(n, false);\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!vis[i]) { count++; dfs(i, adj, vis); }\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nPairwise connectivity testing is O(V^3). Using DFS to traverse and mark entire connected components reduces the workload to O(V + E), which is significantly faster for large graph structures."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nvoid naiveAPSP(int n, int adj[500][500]) {\n\t// Inefficiently search for all paths\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid floydWarshall(int n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>& dist) {\n\tfor (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n\t\t\t\tif (dist[i][k] < 1e9 && dist[k][j] < 1e9)\n\t\t\t\t\tdist[i][j] = std::min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);\n}\n---\nFloyd-Warshall's O(V^3) algorithm is preferred over naive all-pairs searches because it has a lower constant factor and utilizes the CPU cache more effectively, making it the standard for dense all-pairs shortest path problems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid traverse(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) traverse(v, adj);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid traverse(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {\n\tvisited[u] = true;\n\tfor (int v : adj[u]) if (!visited[v]) traverse(v, adj, visited);\n}\n---\nMissing a visited check allows the algorithm to re-visit nodes indefinitely on cycles or redundantly in DAGs. Introducing a visited array ensures each edge and node is touched once, optimizing the search to linear O(V + E) time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint shortest(int s, int t, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q; q.push({s, 0});\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tauto [u, d] = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return d;\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) q.push({v, d + 1});\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nint shortest(int s, int t, int n, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj) {\n\tstd::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n\tdist[s] = 0; std::queue<int> q; q.push(s);\n\twhile (!q.empty()) {\n\t\tint u = q.front(); q.pop();\n\t\tif (u == t) return dist[u];\n\t\tfor (int v : adj[u]) {\n\t\t\tif (dist[v] == -1) {\n\t\t\t\tdist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nWithout a visited check, the queue can grow exponentially as nodes are re-enqueued via different paths. Tracking visited nodes with a distance array keeps the complexity at O(V + E) and ensures linear memory consumption."}
